  "title": "Islamic law (sharia)",
  "summary": "In the early eighth century, groups of pious specialists formed the ancient schools of Islamic law, among them those of Kufa, Basra, Medina, Mecca and Syria. In his Risalah (815–820), al-Shafi'i set out four sources of the law: the Quran, the Sunnah, consensus and analogy. Major schools today include the Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'i and Hanbali, and the Ja'fari, the main Shia school. About half of Muslim-majority countries have sharia-based laws, typically on marriage, divorce, inheritance and child custody.",
